WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:14.000
Ok, let's start from the beginning, so when the ACUs were wired to all the sensors and

00:14.000 --> 00:20.000
actuators and motors in the vehicle in order to provide functions.

00:20.000 --> 00:32.000
Each sensor and actuator were connected and wired to an ACU will have more weight and

00:32.000 --> 00:39.000
costs for the vehicle, so there was the necessity to wire together also the ACUs to minimize

00:39.000 --> 00:45.000
the number of wires needed in a vehicle.

00:45.000 --> 00:53.000
So, the ACUs started to send and receive data via network.

00:53.000 --> 01:00.000
The need for a robust and stable communication protocol raises because in modern vehicles

01:00.000 --> 01:08.000
there is the need to connect a large number of ACUs to each other and there is the necessity

01:08.000 --> 01:14.000
to connect also ACUs that are safety relevant for the driver.

01:14.000 --> 01:19.000
So, no information loss should happen during this communication.

01:19.000 --> 01:28.000
For example, the braking ACUs could not detect wheels that are spinning, that are slipping

01:28.000 --> 01:35.000
and signal it to the ACUs that can, to the engine control units that can temporarily reduce

01:35.000 --> 01:36.000
the torque output.

01:36.000 --> 01:43.000
This is relevant information for the safety of the drivers, so we must be sure that this

01:43.000 --> 01:49.000
information shared via network is not corrupted in any way.

01:49.000 --> 01:56.000
So, just one point, I am recording this lesson.

01:56.000 --> 01:58.000
Are there any problems for you?

01:58.000 --> 02:02.000
I didn't tell you at the beginning, sorry.

02:02.000 --> 02:03.000
No problem.

02:03.000 --> 02:04.000
Okay.

02:04.000 --> 02:09.000
So, we are recording.

02:09.000 --> 02:10.000
Okay.

02:10.000 --> 02:19.000
So, networks and network protocols allow the ECU to share information quickly with the reduced

02:19.000 --> 02:23.000
risk of undetected errors.

02:23.000 --> 02:33.000
Of course, this communication network must be robust in terms also of EMC immunity, electromagnetic

02:33.000 --> 02:34.000
disturbed immunity.

02:34.000 --> 02:43.000
For example, the ignition system used for the combustion engines can create a lot of interference,

02:43.000 --> 02:54.000
and so we need to design special vehicle technologies, and the use of a digital communication bus means

02:54.000 --> 03:01.000
that this interference can be detected and corrected automatically.

03:01.000 --> 03:12.000
The network used between ECU and the vehicle EE systems are based on serial digital communication.

03:12.000 --> 03:21.000
So, to meet all those needs, CAN network was developed, and it is the most used one in the automotive field.

03:21.000 --> 03:34.000
We will also see that the LIM network, the second type of network we will face today, is, let me say, a more economic alternative to the CAN.

03:34.000 --> 03:35.000
Okay.

03:35.000 --> 03:39.000
Let's go deep into the CAN network.

03:39.000 --> 03:48.000
CAN, C-A-N, is an autonomous, used for controller area network, also known as CAN bus.

03:48.000 --> 03:53.000
It is a serial standard bus, mainly used in the automotive field.

03:53.000 --> 04:07.000
The CAN communication protocol is a multicast type, used to connect different electronic ECU, electronic control unit, that from now we will call ACU, in the vehicle.

04:07.000 --> 04:20.000
The CAN has been especially designed to operate without any problems, even in an electromagnetically disturbed environment, as the vehicle can be used to the interconnection of a lot of ACU sensors,

04:20.000 --> 04:26.000
actuators, antennas, because it uses a balanced differential potential line.

04:26.000 --> 04:36.000
So, uh, and EMC noise immunity can be also, um, increased by using a twisted layer of cables.

04:36.000 --> 04:37.000
I don't think so.

04:37.000 --> 04:38.000
I don't think so.

04:38.000 --> 04:39.000
I don't think so.

04:39.000 --> 04:40.000
I don't think so.

04:40.000 --> 04:41.000
Sorry.

04:41.000 --> 04:42.000
Can you hear me?

04:42.000 --> 04:47.000
Am I going too fast?

04:47.000 --> 04:49.000
There are audio problems.

04:49.000 --> 04:50.000
No, it's okay.

04:50.000 --> 04:51.000
It's okay?

04:51.000 --> 04:59.000
Yes, but I think sometimes there is an internal connection problem.

04:59.000 --> 05:00.000
Oh.

05:00.000 --> 05:06.000
With my internet connection, let me check.

05:06.000 --> 05:07.000
Okay.

05:07.000 --> 05:22.000
Please interrupt me in any moment if I'm going too fast or if something is missing or something is not clear, okay?

05:22.000 --> 05:23.000
Okay.

05:23.000 --> 05:41.000
So, um, the, the, um, the operating voltage level of the CAN bus, okay, let's check the, the first slide, no, the, the main features of the CAN protocol.

05:41.000 --> 05:42.000
Okay.

05:42.000 --> 05:49.000
The first one we need to consider is the multicast communication, multi-master access to the bus.

05:49.000 --> 05:53.000
That means that on the CAN network there are, there is no master and slave protocol.

05:53.000 --> 06:01.000
All the ACU can communicate, uh, whatever they want on the network and all the ACU can read information shared on the bus.

06:01.000 --> 06:08.000
And all the ACU are able to communicate in the same, uh, at the same time, in the same moment, okay?

06:08.000 --> 06:15.000
And the second main feature we need to, uh, consider of the CAN network is the error detection, uh, strategy.

06:15.000 --> 06:22.000
Uh, we will see in the next slide that the CAN has a robust, uh, error management strategy.

06:22.000 --> 06:36.000
Uh, and it means that the, the protocol is able to identify if an ACU has a communication problem and, uh, is able to exclude from the bus the ACU that has the communication problem

06:36.000 --> 06:41.000
in order to not affect the status of all the CAN bus.

06:41.000 --> 06:46.000
Okay.

06:46.000 --> 06:56.000
The interface between the CAN network, uh, the, and the ACU, the, the, the, the, the, the bus and the ACU connected on the bus,

06:56.000 --> 07:05.000
uh, is typically, uh, a, a special type of amplifier called the transceiver, that, uh, that means transmitter and receiver.

07:05.000 --> 07:09.000
The CAN network is composed by, sorry, two wires, okay?

07:09.000 --> 07:19.000
As we say at the beginning, we have a two wire with differential voltage, uh, on the bus, and all the ACU are connected in parallel on this bus.

07:19.000 --> 07:24.000
So, we have the ACU connected via transceiver to the bus.

07:24.000 --> 07:33.000
The transceiver, the CAN transceiver, uh, converts the electrical representation of a bit in the one used by the controller.

07:33.000 --> 07:36.000
So, to the one defined for the bus.

07:36.000 --> 07:44.000
We will see it in detail, the physical, uh, layer of the CAN in detail in the next slide.

07:44.000 --> 07:57.000
It also, the, the transceiver must also provide a, a, a, a sufficient output current to drive the bus electrical state when the ACU wants to transmit on the network.

07:57.000 --> 08:07.000
So, the ACU that wants to transmit on the CAN network will force the voltage level on the bus, uh, and, uh, with the, via transceiver.

08:07.000 --> 08:24.000
And, in receiver mode, as a receiver, the transceiver provides the recessive signal level, and it, it, it is also used to protect the chip, the, the internal chip of the ACU, uh, against excessive voltage on the bus line.

08:24.000 --> 08:36.000
Okay, and this is also able to, um, identify electrical level on the lines, uh, such as breakage or short circuit and so on.

08:36.000 --> 08:49.000
Let's see now how the data, uh, transfer process works when we say we have a multi-master bus where all the ACU can, can write and read information.

08:49.000 --> 08:52.000
Okay, we have the two-wire bus.

08:52.000 --> 08:57.000
We will call from now the CANH and CANL.

08:57.000 --> 09:03.000
H is the, the line to high potential, L is low, so CANH and CANL.

09:03.000 --> 09:06.000
And we have all the ACU connected in parallel.

09:06.000 --> 09:08.000
In this example, we only have three ACU.

09:08.000 --> 09:09.000
Okay?

09:09.000 --> 09:12.000
Let's see how the transfer data works.

09:12.000 --> 09:21.000
The CANNode1, uh, the ACU can also be called node, or nodes of the network.

09:21.000 --> 09:24.000
The ACU number one provide data.

09:24.000 --> 09:26.000
What does it mean provide data?

09:26.000 --> 09:30.000
Maybe the ACU number one has, uh, let me do a practical example.

09:30.000 --> 09:31.000
Okay?

09:31.000 --> 09:38.000
The ACU number one is connected with an, uh, uh, as an external temperature sensor connected.

09:38.000 --> 09:39.000
Okay?

09:39.000 --> 09:46.000
So, the ACU number one is able to read the data of the external temperature from this sensor.

09:46.000 --> 09:49.000
But all the other ACU are not able to, to read this data.

09:49.000 --> 09:58.000
So, ACU number one will share it on the CAN network in order, uh, so that all the other ACU can read this data.

09:58.000 --> 10:06.000
Because the external temperature data, for example, is, uh, useful also for the other ACU functionality.

10:06.000 --> 10:16.000
Um, for example, the climate control unit, in order to perform automatic climate functionalities, needs to know the external temperature.

10:16.000 --> 10:21.000
But maybe the climate control unit has no external temperature sensor connected.

10:21.000 --> 10:29.000
So, you have to image that an ACU, uh, is connected to sensors, actuators, and also with a network.

10:29.000 --> 10:34.000
So, the ACU number one has some data that we want to share on the bus.

10:34.000 --> 10:39.000
Because those data can be useful also for the other ACU's functionalities.

10:39.000 --> 10:45.000
So, the ACU number one provides data, and then we send this data on the bus.

10:45.000 --> 10:48.000
The ACU number two receives the data.

10:48.000 --> 10:51.000
So, reads those data on the bus.

10:51.000 --> 10:53.000
We'll check the data.

10:53.000 --> 10:56.000
We will see later, uh, in detail this check.

10:56.000 --> 11:01.000
But, uh, mainly the check is, that the ACU number two will perform is,

11:01.000 --> 11:05.000
is this data useful for my functionalities?

11:05.000 --> 11:08.000
If yes, then we'll accept the data.

11:08.000 --> 11:12.000
If not, as ACU number three, we'll ignore the data.

11:12.000 --> 11:17.000
So, this is the meaning of multi-master, of broadcast communication.

11:17.000 --> 11:23.000
All the ACU will share some data on the campus, and the other ACU, if those data are useful,

11:23.000 --> 11:29.000
we'll read it, and, uh, we'll consider those data, or we'll ignore the data.

11:29.000 --> 11:32.000
We'll ignore the message that the first ACU is sending.

11:32.000 --> 11:33.000
Okay?

11:33.000 --> 11:45.000
Oh, here you can find, um, a recap of the main, the most used, uh, bit rate, so speed of the network,

11:45.000 --> 11:49.000
uh, related to the bus length.

11:49.000 --> 11:58.000
Of course, we have a relationship between the transmission speed, so the propagation delay of the signal, uh, and the bus length.

11:58.000 --> 12:03.000
So, in this table, you can see the mainly used bus length with the corresponded bit time.

12:03.000 --> 12:04.000
Okay?

12:04.000 --> 12:12.000
The most common used one are 250 kilobits per second, and 500 kilobits per second.

12:12.000 --> 12:13.000
Okay?

12:13.000 --> 12:16.000
But that's only, uh, an example.

12:16.000 --> 12:23.000
Okay, let's see the, how, uh, physically is composed, is, is, uh, is made the cam network.

12:23.000 --> 12:30.000
Let's, for now, let's just ignore the low speed network, the second picture, you can see in this slide.

12:30.000 --> 12:35.000
Also, because the low speed cam network are no longer used in the automotive field.

12:35.000 --> 12:39.000
So, we will have a focus on the high speed network.

12:39.000 --> 12:44.000
As you can see, we have a two-wire bus, all the ACU connected in parallel.

12:44.000 --> 12:49.000
Here, in this schematic, you can see TX and RX.

12:49.000 --> 12:55.000
Those two amplifiers compose the transceiver that we, as we said before.

12:55.000 --> 13:04.000
So, the interface between the physical network and the ACU is this transceiver in transmitter and receiver mode

13:04.000 --> 13:12.000
that can convert the electrical signals on the bus into a logical signal used by the ACU.

13:12.000 --> 13:17.000
Because we need to know that we have a physical transmission on the network.

13:17.000 --> 13:23.000
If we connect an oscilloscope on the, okay, network, we will only see voltage drops.

13:23.000 --> 13:24.000
Okay?

13:24.000 --> 13:32.000
We will see the differential voltage of these two wires that change, uh, based on the message on the ACU that are transmitting.

13:32.000 --> 13:39.000
But the ACU, the controller, doesn't use electrical signals to communicate.

13:39.000 --> 13:44.000
The ACU will use logical representation of these electrical signals.

13:44.000 --> 13:46.000
So, this is the main feature of the transceiver.

13:46.000 --> 13:50.000
The transceiver will translate the electrical signals into logical one.

13:50.000 --> 13:53.000
Later, we will see the details also on this.

13:53.000 --> 13:57.000
So, we have the two wires, the ACU connected in parallel.

13:57.000 --> 14:06.000
And then we have also two termination resistors of 120 ohms at both the ends of the network.

14:06.000 --> 14:12.000
Oh, I'm moving the mouse around the scale.

14:12.000 --> 14:13.000
Okay.

14:13.000 --> 14:17.000
Here you can see 120 ohms termination resistors.

14:17.000 --> 14:18.000
Okay?

14:18.000 --> 14:31.000
If we check the resistance of the can at the end of the line or in any points of the can line, we will read the parallel of those two resistors.

14:31.000 --> 14:33.000
So, 60 ohms.

14:33.000 --> 14:34.000
What does it mean?

14:34.000 --> 14:48.000
If I read the resistance on any point of the can line, I will read 60 ohms if the can network is correctly terminated, is correctly closed.

14:48.000 --> 14:56.000
If I will read 120 ohms, means that one of the two resistors is missing and the can is open.

14:56.000 --> 14:59.000
We will say the can is open.

14:59.000 --> 15:07.000
The function of those two resistors is to avoid signals reflection on the bus line.

15:07.000 --> 15:20.000
Because, as we said before, the can line can be a very long communication bus that can interconnect also ACU that are on the opposite side of the vehicle.

15:20.000 --> 15:31.000
So, we need to avoid signal reflections on this line and this is why we terminate, we close the can network on two 120 ohms resistors.

15:31.000 --> 15:39.000
Of course, the 120 ohms value is a value that is described in the standard.

15:39.000 --> 15:44.000
The can protocol, of course, is compliant to this standard.

15:44.000 --> 15:45.000
Okay?

15:45.000 --> 15:59.000
So, electrically we have the ACU connected in parallel on the two line can bus and two ACU and two resistors of 120 ohms at both the ends of the can network.

15:59.000 --> 16:10.000
Okay, this is what I was saying before regarding the logical and physical encoding of the can signals.

16:10.000 --> 16:23.000
Of course, as we said, the ACU will read and will communicate with the logical states.

16:23.000 --> 16:29.000
So, zero and one logical states are identified for the ACU communication.

16:29.000 --> 16:39.000
In the can protocol, we will say that the zero is the dominant and the one bit is the recessive state.

16:39.000 --> 16:40.000
Okay?

16:40.000 --> 16:47.000
Here you can see we have the differential voltage between can H and can low.

16:47.000 --> 16:54.000
We say that the can network works with the differential voltage, so the difference between can H and can L.

16:54.000 --> 16:58.000
So, one thing that I didn't say before.

16:58.000 --> 17:11.000
Of course, why is the can network that is robust in terms of electromagnetically disturbed environment, EMC noise?

17:11.000 --> 17:14.000
Why as in EMC noise immunity?

17:14.000 --> 17:16.000
Because it uses a differential voltage.

17:16.000 --> 17:29.000
So, if we have a disturbance on the lines, on the two lines, of course, this disturbance will be removed, will be cancelled by the difference of the voltage between can H and can L.

17:29.000 --> 17:43.000
Okay, so, we can have the bus voltage set to recessive if the differential voltage is inside those thresholds or dominant if it is in those thresholds.

17:43.000 --> 17:45.000
Here you can see the voltage values.

17:45.000 --> 17:57.000
What is important is that when an ACU forces on the bus a low voltage, we will have a representation of a logical one.

17:57.000 --> 18:05.000
If we have a high voltage difference, then we will have the dominant, the zero logical state.

18:05.000 --> 18:10.000
This is how physically the communication works.

18:10.000 --> 18:24.000
The ACU will force a certain voltage level on the bus, a certain differential voltage level on the bus, and the transceiver will translate it into a sequence of logical zero and one.

18:24.000 --> 18:32.000
So, logical zero is the dominant state, we will call it dominant, and one is the recessive state.

18:32.000 --> 18:36.000
This is how physically works the can communication.

18:36.000 --> 18:45.000
But today we will have a focus, not on the can physical layer, let me say, but on the can protocol, on the communication.

18:45.000 --> 18:49.000
And so, what does an ACU share on the network?

18:49.000 --> 18:55.000
An ACU will communicate and share on the network some data packets that we will call messages.

18:55.000 --> 18:56.000
Okay?

18:56.000 --> 18:59.000
So, the ACU sends messages on the network.

18:59.000 --> 19:05.000
We have four different types of frames, of course, according to the content.

19:05.000 --> 19:15.000
The one on which we will have a focus today are the data frames, that are the frames that contain the data.

19:15.000 --> 19:16.000
Okay?

19:16.000 --> 19:24.000
So, the data that we say that the ACU wants to share on the network will be contained in the data frame.

19:28.000 --> 19:32.000
Then we can have remote frame, error frame, and overload frames.

19:32.000 --> 19:36.000
Remote frames, not mostly used in the automotive field.

19:36.000 --> 19:43.000
The error frame, we will have a focus later on the error management and so on the error frames, are error messages.

19:43.000 --> 19:49.000
Are special frames transmitted only when there is a communication fault on the bus.

19:49.000 --> 20:02.000
Then we have overload frames that are frames used to require an additional time delay in the transmission when the bus is overloaded.

20:02.000 --> 20:09.000
As we said before, the CAN network is a multi-master and broadcast bus, digital bus.

20:09.000 --> 20:14.000
So, all the ACU can communicate at the same time.

20:14.000 --> 20:21.000
But, of course, we have some constraints, some design constraints on the CAN network.

20:21.000 --> 20:35.000
On the CAN network, the protocol says that the CAN network must be designed in order to operate with a maximum bus load of 13-14%.

20:35.000 --> 20:41.000
So, how the ACU are able to communicate all together, all in the same time.

20:41.000 --> 20:54.000
Because we design the CAN network in order to have not the 100% of the bus load full, but we will have only a part of the bus load.

20:54.000 --> 21:05.000
The overload frames are used when we have a bus overload, and so the transmission is not guaranteed.

21:05.000 --> 21:06.000
Ok.

21:06.000 --> 21:17.000
Let's see the data frames that are the most used and the most important ones, and are the ones that you will, of course, see during your life.

21:17.000 --> 21:24.000
This is the data frame structure. We have a start and an end of frames.

21:24.000 --> 21:36.000
That are only some delimiters, let me say, of the messages, and are identifiers for the other issues in order to understand where a message starts and when it ends.

21:36.000 --> 21:45.000
Those are mainly some standard patterns of bits that identify the start and the end of the message.

21:45.000 --> 21:54.000
Then we have the message identifier, the control field, the data field, the CRC field, and the acknowledge field, the hack field.

21:54.000 --> 21:59.000
Let's see it in detail.

21:59.000 --> 22:11.000
The data frame identifier, the first field, contains the message identifier that can be, for the protocol, 11 or 29 bits.

22:11.000 --> 22:16.000
We have two types of identifier formats, the standard one and the extended one.

22:16.000 --> 22:21.000
Why to choose one or the other? No particular reason.

22:21.000 --> 22:26.000
It is a user option, let me say, to choose the standard or the extended one.

22:26.000 --> 22:36.000
Of course, if we use the standard format of identifier, we have to know that on the same can network we cannot have two messages with the same identifier.

22:36.000 --> 22:40.000
The identifier, let me say, is the name of the message.

22:40.000 --> 22:49.000
The field that is used from the other ACU in order to understand if this message is useful or not.

22:49.000 --> 22:54.000
If the ACU that is receiving this message must read this message or not.

22:54.000 --> 22:55.000
Ok?

22:55.000 --> 23:08.000
So, 11 bits standard identifier means that we have two raised to 11 possibilities, possible messages, different messages identifiers on the network.

23:08.000 --> 23:16.000
29 bits identifier means we have two raised to 29 possible identifiers to choose on the network.

23:16.000 --> 23:20.000
So, this is the only criteria.

23:20.000 --> 23:23.000
So, in the first field we will have the message identifier.

23:23.000 --> 23:27.000
The field that identifies the name of the message.

23:27.000 --> 23:32.000
We will understand it deeply later when we will see the network specification.

23:32.000 --> 23:41.000
Then we have the data field that can be from 1 to 8 bytes long.

23:41.000 --> 23:44.000
So, up to 64 bits.

23:44.000 --> 23:49.000
The data field is the field that contains the information.

23:49.000 --> 23:50.000
Ok?

23:50.000 --> 23:53.000
So, in this field the ACU will share the information.

23:53.000 --> 23:56.000
The external temperature, the vehicle speed and so on.

23:56.000 --> 24:07.000
Then we have the control, the CRC and the ACK knowledge field that are three fields used for the data consistency control.

24:07.000 --> 24:17.000
So, at the beginning of the lesson we said that the CAN network is designed to be, to have a strong error management strategy, error detection strategy.

24:17.000 --> 24:29.000
And in order to guarantee that the data messages, that as we said before, can be also, can contain also information, safety relevant for the driver.

24:29.000 --> 24:32.000
Those data are not corrupted in any way.

24:32.000 --> 24:40.000
During the transmission the data must not be lost or corrupted in some way.

24:40.000 --> 24:47.000
So, those three fields are used in order to control the consistency of the data field.

24:47.000 --> 24:48.000
Let's check.

24:48.000 --> 24:50.000
The first one, the control field.

24:50.000 --> 24:51.000
There is a slide.

24:51.000 --> 24:52.000
Ok.

24:52.000 --> 24:59.000
In the control field we will find the, let's just ignore the reserved bit.

24:59.000 --> 25:03.000
We will have 4 bits of data length code.

25:03.000 --> 25:08.000
So, in this field is declared the length of the data field.

25:08.000 --> 25:09.000
Ok?

25:09.000 --> 25:17.000
So, if the data field will contain 8 bytes of information, in the control field we will have the information 8.

25:17.000 --> 25:18.000
Ok?

25:18.000 --> 25:26.000
So, it's a field used to control if the data length code is, the DLC is correct or not.

25:26.000 --> 25:31.000
Because, let me do a practical example in order to better understand.

25:31.000 --> 25:49.000
Now, if an ACU receives a message and in the control field is declared that this message has 8 bytes length, but I am receiving, I am an ACU that is receiving this message and I check the data field and I see only 6 bytes, for example.

25:49.000 --> 25:56.000
So, it means that somewhere we lost 2 bytes of data.

25:56.000 --> 25:57.000
The message is corrupted.

25:57.000 --> 25:58.000
It is not ok.

25:58.000 --> 25:59.000
Ok.

25:59.000 --> 26:04.000
So, this is a first check that the ACU that receives a message performs.

26:04.000 --> 26:07.000
So, control field, data length code.

26:07.000 --> 26:14.000
Then we have the CRC field.

26:14.000 --> 26:15.000
Let me check.

26:15.000 --> 26:16.000
I think there is a slide.

26:16.000 --> 26:17.000
Yes.

26:17.000 --> 26:19.000
In this field, there is a number.

26:19.000 --> 26:26.000
This number is the division of a known polynomial that we will call the generator.

26:26.000 --> 26:29.000
The calculation of the 8 byte CRC is a standard calculation.

26:29.000 --> 26:30.000
Ok?

26:30.000 --> 26:44.000
So, we have this generator polynomial that is a standard one and we will create another polynomial starting from the data field.

26:44.000 --> 26:53.000
The reminder of the division between the polynomial generated from the data field that you have to image as a sequence of 0 and 1.

26:53.000 --> 26:55.000
So, this is the data field.

26:55.000 --> 26:58.000
From this sequence, we will create a polynomial.

26:58.000 --> 27:03.000
We will divide it for the generator and we will have a certain polynomial divisional result.

27:03.000 --> 27:04.000
Ok?

27:04.000 --> 27:05.000
A certain number.

27:05.000 --> 27:09.000
The reminder of the division will be an integral number.

27:09.000 --> 27:20.000
This calculation is performed both from the ACU that is sending the message and from the ACU that is receiving the message.

27:20.000 --> 27:21.000
Ok?

27:21.000 --> 27:32.000
So, if this calculation is the same for the ACU that is sending the message and for the ACU that is receiving the message, then the CRC check is ok.

27:32.000 --> 27:55.000
If this reminder is different from the ACU that is sending and the ACU that is receiving the message, it means that the generator is the same for both, but it means that the data field that has arrived to the ACU that is receiving the message is different from the one that the ACU has sent.

27:55.000 --> 27:59.000
So, somewhere the data has been corrupted.

27:59.000 --> 28:04.000
So, CRC check is another level of check of the data consistency.

28:04.000 --> 28:05.000
Ok?

28:05.000 --> 28:17.000
So, polynomial division between the polynomial generated from the data field and a known and standard polynomial that we call generator.

28:17.000 --> 28:18.000
Ok?

28:18.000 --> 28:27.000
The result of this division must be the same between the ACU that is sending the data and the ACU that is receiving the data.

28:27.000 --> 28:30.000
So, we have two levels of control until now.

28:30.000 --> 28:33.000
The first one on the length of the data field.

28:33.000 --> 28:38.000
The second one on the consistency of the data field.

28:38.000 --> 28:39.000
Ok?

28:39.000 --> 28:41.000
Then we have the ACK knowledge field.

28:41.000 --> 28:48.000
The ACK knowledge field is always set to, is a field that is always set to recessive.

28:48.000 --> 28:55.000
Ok, I think here is described as ACK field.

28:55.000 --> 28:56.000
Ok.

28:56.000 --> 29:06.000
The ACK field will be set to recessive from the ACU that is sending the message.

29:06.000 --> 29:16.000
And will be set to dominant state from the ACU that is receiving the messages when the message has been received.

29:16.000 --> 29:18.000
Let me explain it better.

29:18.000 --> 29:19.000
Ok.

29:19.000 --> 29:22.000
We have the ACU that is, that sends a message on the network.

29:22.000 --> 29:26.000
This message can be read from one or more ACU.

29:26.000 --> 29:33.000
When the ACU sends the message on the network, the ACK knowledge field is set to recessive state.

29:33.000 --> 29:34.000
So, to one.

29:34.000 --> 29:45.000
If at least one ACU receives this message, then we will, let me say, the flag will set this field to dominant.

29:45.000 --> 29:46.000
So, to zero.

29:46.000 --> 29:56.000
And in order to let to the ACU that has been sending this message on the network, let me know that the message has been correctly received.

29:56.000 --> 30:02.000
So, the ACU that is sending the message knows that the message has been arrived.

30:02.000 --> 30:04.000
In some way has arrived on the network.

30:04.000 --> 30:05.000
Ok?

30:05.000 --> 30:17.000
From the ACK knowledge field, we will check, we will start the part of error confinement mechanism of the scan protocol.

30:17.000 --> 30:30.000
Because, we say that the control field and the CRC field are used to control the consistency and the length of the data message, of the data field of the message.

30:30.000 --> 30:40.000
The ACK knowledge field is used from the ACU that is sending the message in order to understand if she is correctly sending messages on the network.

30:40.000 --> 30:41.000
Every ACU has two counters.

30:41.000 --> 30:47.000
Every ACK has two counters, two internal counters.

30:47.000 --> 30:51.000
The transmit error counter and the receive error counter.

30:51.000 --> 30:58.000
Those counters are incremented and decremented checking the ACK knowledge field.

30:58.000 --> 31:11.000
If an ACU that is transmitting a message, the first time that the ACU transmits the message, the ACK knowledge field is not flagged by any other ACU on the network.

31:11.000 --> 31:21.000
The transmit error counter will increase of 8 because one transmission of the message has not gone good.

31:21.000 --> 31:28.000
The ACU are not receiving what the ACU thinks that is transmitting on the network.

31:28.000 --> 31:40.000
If another message is sent and some ACU correctly flags the ACK knowledge field, then this transmitter or counter decrease of 1.

31:40.000 --> 31:49.000
So, plus 8 for each message where the ACK knowledge field has not been set to dominant from another ACU,

31:49.000 --> 31:56.000
decreases of 1 for each time that the ACU is correctly sending messages on the network.

31:56.000 --> 32:03.000
So, the ACK knowledge field has been set to dominant from at least one ACU that will receive this message.

32:03.000 --> 32:12.000
With these rules, plus 8 minus 1 and so on, we define a threshold.

32:12.000 --> 32:24.000
If the transmitter or counter raises above 255 thresholds, we will say that the ACU is in bus of state.

32:24.000 --> 32:25.000
What does it mean?

32:25.000 --> 32:26.000
What does it mean?

32:26.000 --> 32:39.000
If the counter raises 255, it means that at least 32 times, because 32, yes, per 8 is 255.

32:39.000 --> 32:51.000
For 32 consecutive times, the ACU tries to send a message on the network, but no other ACU has been flagged the ACK knowledge field.

32:51.000 --> 32:58.000
So, the ACU thinks that it is correctly transmitting on the network, but no other ACU is receiving this message.

32:58.000 --> 33:09.000
So, there is a problem internal to the ACU or on the transceiver of this ACU that is not permitting to the ACU to share the message on the network.

33:09.000 --> 33:14.000
The message is not, let me say, exiting from the transceiver of the ACU.

33:14.000 --> 33:16.000
No ACU is receiving this message.

33:16.000 --> 33:30.000
So, when we raise this threshold, so, plus 8, plus 8, plus 8, after 32 consecutive times that the ACU is trying to send a message on the network with no result on the acknowledged field,

33:30.000 --> 33:34.000
the ACU that is transmitting will go in bus of state.

33:34.000 --> 33:51.000
So, the ACU will exit from the bus, will no longer try to transmit on the bus, will reset, let me say, herself, will restart herself, and then we will try to transmit again.

33:51.000 --> 34:04.000
But, at the first transmission, after the bus of state, the bus of time, that is one second medium for the ACU, so, one second of silence in bus of state,

34:04.000 --> 34:16.000
then I will try again to send message, if at the first attempt the acknowledge field is still to recessive state and no ACU is receiving this message,

34:16.000 --> 34:25.000
will remain in bus of state until the transmitter on counter will go below this threshold.

34:25.000 --> 34:26.000
Okay?

34:26.000 --> 34:38.000
So, the acknowledge field is used from the ACU that is transmitting in order to understand if the message that she thinks that is transmitting is really arrived on the bus.

34:38.000 --> 34:40.000
Someone has really received this message.

34:40.000 --> 34:41.000
Okay?

34:41.000 --> 34:48.000
So, quick recap on the data message structure.

34:48.000 --> 35:00.000
As we said before, start and end of frame, some kind of delimiters to make it clear where a message start and a message ends.

35:00.000 --> 35:20.000
Message identifier, 11 or 29 bits format, that identifies the name of the message, let me say, and is used from the ACU that receives the messages in order to know if this specific message with this specific identifier is useful or not.

35:20.000 --> 35:29.000
If this message, the ACU that receives this message has to read this message or can ignore it.

35:29.000 --> 35:33.000
And this is the check that we saw at the beginning.

35:33.000 --> 35:34.000
Okay?

35:34.000 --> 35:37.000
So, the ACU sends a message with a certain identifier.

35:37.000 --> 35:40.000
The ACU number one will check the message identifier.

35:40.000 --> 35:45.000
If this message is useful for its functionalities, we'll accept this data.

35:45.000 --> 35:47.000
Then we'll ignore this data.

35:47.000 --> 35:56.000
And we'll also check the consistency of the data looking at the control and CRC field.

35:56.000 --> 36:09.000
So, control field, as we said before, declares the data length code, the DLC of the message, the length of the data field.

36:09.000 --> 36:12.000
That can be from 1 to 8 bytes long.

36:12.000 --> 36:14.000
So, 64 bits.

36:14.000 --> 36:27.000
The CRC field is used from the ACU that is receiving the message in order to, and also from the ACU that is sending messages, in order to check the consistency of the data field.

36:27.000 --> 36:39.000
Because it is only the reminder of division between a known polynomial, a standard polynomial, and the polynomial generated by the data from the data field.

36:39.000 --> 36:52.000
This reminder, if is the same reminder calculated from the ACU that is receiving this message, and from the one that is transmitting this message, then the data field is okay.

36:52.000 --> 37:04.000
If this result is different between the calculation of the ACU that is sending and the ACU that is receiving this message, then we have a problem on the data field.

37:04.000 --> 37:07.000
Somewhere the data field has been corrupted.

37:07.000 --> 37:19.000
And then we have the acknowledge field, that is the field used from the ACU that is transmitting this message, in order to understand if the message has been correctly sent on the network.

