40:08.000 --> 40:20.000
Something that I didn't say to you before is the transmitting method. So, we have mainly three methods of transmission on CAN.

40:20.000 --> 40:31.000
We can have cyclic messages, periodic on-event messages, or periodic on-event messages.

40:31.000 --> 40:43.000
The first, the most used ones are the cyclic messages, what we will see here as CX transmitter method cyclic.

40:43.000 --> 40:58.000
It means that this message is sent from the Airbag ACU each 100 milliseconds, okay, with a periodicity of 100 milliseconds in a cyclic way, okay?

40:58.000 --> 41:08.000
We can also have messages that, in the database, you will find that no-message-send type method that are on-event messages.

41:08.000 --> 41:28.000
On-event means that this specific message, the message that is called CSG data code response nub, will not be always present on the network, will only be, the ACU will send it only if a specific event is happening.

41:28.000 --> 41:36.000
For example, this specific message is sent each time there is a key-off to key-on transition.

41:36.000 --> 41:50.000
So, when the key passes from off to on, when we power on the vehicle, we will see this message only for three samples after the key-off to key-on transition.

41:50.000 --> 42:00.000
In a normal state, in a normal state, when we are in key-on from, I don't know, ten minutes, this message will not be, will no longer be present on the network, okay?

42:00.000 --> 42:12.000
So, on-event messages are messages that are sent only for a certain number of times, only when a specific event is happening in the vehicle.

42:12.000 --> 42:24.000
We mean, when I say events, I mean a specific vehicle condition, for example, the key that passes from off to on.

42:24.000 --> 42:36.000
Instead of a cyclic message that is always present on the network, we always with the same periodicity that is defined here in the field cycle time of the DBC.

42:36.000 --> 42:45.000
Here, the cycle time is expressed in milliseconds, okay? So, here we have 100 milliseconds, 1 second, and so on.

42:45.000 --> 42:54.000
And you can see no message sent type, so the event message, on-event message, has a cycle time of 0 milliseconds.

42:54.000 --> 43:02.000
Then we have a third transmission method, that is COC, or cyclic on change.

43:02.000 --> 43:18.000
So, we have a message that normally is cyclic 1, so as a defined periodicity, but if a certain event happens on the vehicle, in the vehicle condition, that this message will double its periodicity, okay?

43:18.000 --> 43:36.000
So, the classical example for the airbag control unit, for example, can be a message that is sent with a periodicity of 1 second, so each transmission after 1 second, if we have a crash of the vehicle, okay?

43:36.000 --> 43:55.000
An accident, so the airbag control unit will double this periodicity, because the time network needs those information, let me say, with a strictly periodicity, needs to update this information with a faster periodicity.

43:55.000 --> 44:12.000
That's why we can have cyclic on change messages, messages that are normally sent with a certain cycle time, but due to a vehicle event, can double, can increase, in general, their periodicity.

